Let's further attempt a formal proof for our own confidence. We can model lex nested absence sets. The only feasible cost-reducing exchange is moving an absence from 
a size-3 group to a size-5 group. Why? In lex, absence sets are nested by book frequency. Any group of size 3 misses the two least frequent books among those not 
present; any size5 misses none. Exchanging one of the missed books creates two size4. Other exchanges would require moving absences of more frequent books out of 
smaller groups, but those absences are already concentrated in the cheapest possible groups (size1/2) due to nested structure; moving them would not reduce total 
loss. This is plausible.

